<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 1px 1px 1px" border-radius="10px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="10px" padding="0px">
    <mj-image
      border-radius="10px 10px 0 0"
      padding="0"
      width="600px"
      src="https://www.londonsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
      alt="Phantom AI funding"
      href="https://www.londonsummary.com/"
    />
    <mj-text
      padding="20px 20px 0px 20px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Phantom AI funding
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="0px 20px 0px 20px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>What’s happening?</strong> The UK government’s AI expansion drive is under scrutiny after an investigation found that some <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/09/revealed-uks-multibillion-ai-drive-is-built-on-phantom-investments" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #80011F; color: black;">major funding may be overstated or unclear</a>. Several AI projects announced since 2024, including new data centres and a UK “sovereign AI supercomputer,” appear to involve rented facilities, early-stage planning, or sites not yet under construction. The multibillion-dollar funding by Nscale and CoreWeave was largely based on company announcements rather than audited commitments.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Why?</strong> Governments are racing to lead in AI and often rely on big funding announcements from tech firms to signal economic growth. However, many AI infrastructure projects are based on future plans, equipment purchases, or leased capacity rather than immediate construction or capital spending. With minimal oversight, governments often accept company estimates without auditing them, while firms inflate figures to boost valuations, partnerships, and political support.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 24px; padding-top: 15px; padding-bottom: 20px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Big picture: </strong>As countries compete for funding from major tech companies, large-scale announcements about data centres, chips, and supercomputers often become part of economic and political messaging. Without clear oversight, governments risk overstating economic gains or relying on projects that may take years to materialize.
</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="20px" />
    
